  "title": "Podhale — the Highlander Land Below the Tatras",
  "lead": "Podhale is the land below the Tatras, cradle of highlander culture. Zakopane, Chochołów, thermal baths. Slow travel through Podhale.",
  "body": "<h2>Why it's worth it</h2><p>Podhale is a historical-ethnographic region at the foot of the Tatras, in the Małopolska voivodeship. This is a region where highlander culture is still alive — music, dance, costume, dialect, cuisine. Podhale is also a region with very old settlement — the first villages were founded here in the 13th and 14th centuries. Podhale villages have distinctive architecture: wooden houses with tall roofs, with a main room and hallway, often with balconies. Add to that wooden churches — among the most valuable in Poland — and wayside shrines.</p><p>Slow travel through Podhale is a journey through a landscape that surprises with its diversity. Podhale has the Tatras, valleys, villages, thermal baths, and towns — all within a few dozen kilometres. It is worth spending 3–5 days here to truly feel this landscape. The best way to explore Podhale is on foot, by bike, or by car — there are about 300 km of hiking and cycling trails. Public transport from Zakopane and Nowy Targ is good (buses, minibuses).</p><h2>What to see</h2><p>Podhale is divided into three main parts: Western Podhale (Zakopane), Central Podhale (Nowy Targ), and Eastern Podhale (Bukowina). Exploring the region takes 3–5 days.</p><h3>Western Podhale</h3><ul><li><strong>Zakopane</strong> — a town at the foot of the Tatras, with Krupówki and Wielka Krokiew. Worth it for culture and as a base.</li><li><strong>Chochołów</strong> — a village with preserved wooden architecture, one of the most valuable in Poland. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Chochołowska Valley</strong> — a valley in the Tatras, with shepherd huts. Worth it for the landscape.</li></ul><h3>Central Podhale</h3><ul><li><strong>Nowy Targ</strong> — a town with a market square and church, the centre of Podhale. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Dębno</strong> — a village with a 15th-century wooden church, a UNESCO site. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Białka Tatrzańska</strong> — a village with thermal baths and ski slopes. Worth it for sport.</li></ul><h3>Eastern Podhale</h3><ul><li><strong>Bukowina Tatrzańska</strong> — a village with preserved architecture and highlander culture. Worth it for culture.</li><li><strong>Jurgów</strong> — a village with a wooden church and open-air museum. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Łapsze Niżne</strong> — a village with a wooden church and castle. Worth it for architecture.</li></ul><h2>When to go</h2><ul><li><strong>May and June</strong> — the best time for slow travel. Warm, green, fewer tourists.</li><li><strong>July–August</strong> — season for trails and highlander festivals. Worth it for events, but crowded in Zakopane.</li><li><strong>September–October</strong> — second best. Autumn colours, fewer people.</li><li><strong>November–April</strong> — Podhale in winter is a paradise for skiers. Worth it for winter sports.</li></ul><h2>Getting there</h2><ul><li><strong>By car:</strong> ~2 hours from Kraków (DK47), ~3 hours from Katowice (DK47), ~5.5 hours from Warsaw (S7 + DK47).</li><li><strong>By train:</strong> Zakopane, Nowy Targ have railway stations, with connections from Kraków and Warsaw.</li><li><strong>By bus:</strong> PKS and private carriers from Kraków and Katowice.</li></ul><p>Podhale is best explored by car or on foot — the region is vast, and public transport between villages can be sparse. Leave the car at the car park in Zakopane.</p><h2>How much time you need</h2><p><strong>A weekend (2 days)</strong> is the minimum — Zakopane, Chochołów.</p><p><strong>Three days</strong> is realistic for a first visit. Zakopane, Chochołów, Nowy Targ, Dębno.</p><p><strong>A week</strong> lets you explore all of Podhale — from Zakopane to Bukowina, with numerous stops.</p><h2>What to eat and where to sleep</h2><ul><li><strong>Kwaśnica</strong> — sour cabbage soup, a highlander classic.</li><li><strong>Oscypek</strong> — smoked sheep cheese, available at shepherd huts.</li><li><strong>Bryndza</strong> — sheep cheese, available at shepherd huts.</li><li><strong>Karpiel</strong> — a highlander dish of cabbage and meat.</li><li><strong>Beer from Podhale breweries</strong> — several craft breweries in the region.</li></ul><p>Accommodation: Zakopane (town, more options), Nowy Targ (town, more options), Bukowina Tatrzańska (village, guesthouses), Białka Tatrzańska (village, guesthouses).</p><h2>Practical tips</h2><ul><li><strong>Guided tours</strong> — highlander culture is rich, take a guide.</li><li><strong>Highlander culture</strong> — pay attention to music, dance, and costume, still alive in many villages.</li><li><strong>Shopping</strong> — buy oscypek, bryndza, and honey directly from highlanders.</li><li><strong>Thermal baths</strong> — the region has several thermal complexes (Termy Chochołowskie, Termy Bania, Termy Bukovina). Book tickets in advance.</li><li><strong>Weather</strong> — Podhale weather can be changeable, even in July bring a jacket.</li><li><strong>Signal</strong> — good throughout the region, weaker in higher mountain sections.</li></ul>",
  "tip": "Visit Chochołów early in the morning — the best time to see the wooden village without crowds and with good light for photos.",
